{"kind":"component","id":"faf557ae-bc40-4397-aa83-04eeb677cebb","type":"b40e1124-e203-4e1a-8962-2a6fb8d751a5","name":"nD","nick":"C30/37","desc":"","box":[953,554,129,168],"pivot":[1020,616],"inputs":[{"id":"8e6f7fdb-2d98-4ec6-93c6-728aa37ac3b0","name":"Material Name","nick":"MatName","box":[955,556,50,20],"src":[]},{"id":"a850f603-4fdf-407e-afa7-d10cc400d47d","name":"E","nick":"E","box":[955,576,50,20],"src":[]},{"id":"601e8943-278f-4ddd-a997-bb64bc7b7310","name":"G","nick":"G","box":[955,596,50,20],"src":[]},{"id":"28958e81-1830-45aa-b145-f926890c9cc1","name":"ν","nick":"ν","box":[955,616,50,20],"src":[]},{"id":"7a1c9165-3533-4b4f-ba99-ca5957f0f89e","name":"Rho","nick":"Rho","box":[955,636,50,20],"src":[]},{"id":"90889a31-be25-45e8-9fd4-26adb673b808","name":"Colour","nick":"Colour","box":[955,656,50,20],"src":[]}],"outputs":[{"id":"ca4c8c32-88f0-4bd0-af0d-6c54d397f29a","name":"Material","nick":"Material","box":[1035,556,45,120],"src":[]}],"unit":"ElasticIsotropic"},
{"kind":"component","id":"81b731ab-6c63-4c04-9ce0-9db3583ecd78","type":"fdfc5598-7aa8-45cf-ae80-2c9996564eb2","name":"SSP Brick (Alpaca4d)","nick":"Vault","desc":"Construct a SSP (Stabilized Single Point) Brick element from an eight vertex mesh.\nThe mesh is reordered into the node order OpenSees wants and checked for a positive Jacobian at every corner - a brick wound the wrong way round, or twisted, is one the solver accepts and answers with the wrong sign rather than rejecting.\n\nA solid has no local axes. OpenSees gives neither element an orientation argument, and an nD material has no orientation either, so the material's own directions - Ex, Ey and Ez on an Elastic Orthotropic - are the global X, Y and Z, and so are the stresses read back. To align an orthotropic material with the part, rotate the model.\nWhere a solid meets a beam or a shell at a point, the two are tied in translation only: a solid node carries no moment, so the junction is a pin.","box":[1077,472,128,84],"pivot":[1144,514],"inputs":[{"id":"f82540af-704e-4923-9d61-635c979412b4","name":"Mesh","nick":"Mesh","box":[1079,474,50,20],"src":["3b07b484-b046-4feb-85fe-bb025fe5b548"]},{"id":"4bf01ff9-8f94-4cb0-bee4-22f1f7a4581b","name":"Material","nick":"Material","box":[1079,494,50,20],"src":["ca4c8c32-88f0-4bd0-af0d-6c54d397f29a"]},{"id":"675098fc-d556-444d-9d4d-dd7c3ed6e8fc","name":"Colour","nick":"Colour","box":[1079,514,50,20],"src":[]},{"id":"f22190ea-2845-4cf6-aa23-09dc153af4b5","name":"ElementId","nick":"ElementId","box":[1079,534,50,20],"src":[]}],"outputs":[{"id":"9aa17a83-eac9-41f1-b680-884be623a516","name":"Element","nick":"Element","box":[1159,474,44,80],"src":[]}]},
{"kind":"component","id":"e67a75b1-8541-41a5-9da5-ef568f87e736","type":"c4e1d7a2-58b6-4f03-8e2a-1b9f6d3a7c05","name":"Support (Alpaca4d)","nick":"Springings","desc":"A support. Rigid: fixes the node along and about the axes of the Position's plane you choose. Elastic: a spring along or about each axis of the plane. Compression Only: holds the node only while it pushes into the support, along the plane's Z, and lets it lift - after a gap, if there is one.\nElastic and Compression Only never fix the node themselves, so a Rigid support on the same point can hold what they leave free: a pinned bearing that can lift is a Compression Only support with a Rigid one holding Tx and Ty only.\nReaction Force gives a spring support a row of its own, with the spring's force.","box":[1081,764,121,188],"pivot":[1140,836],"inputs":[{"id":"2f1432f6-453a-48f1-91e3-a5bccd024fa9","name":"Position","nick":"Position","box":[1083,766,42,20],"src":["1e994b1b-19e1-4f02-8d79-62b95333b28f"]},{"id":"94da88eb-c77c-4d97-a532-8b62c32c0a83","name":"Tx","nick":"Tx","box":[1083,786,42,20],"src":[]},{"id":"c6dced55-bf19-41a6-b37e-ccdc2dae0952","name":"Ty","nick":"Ty","box":[1083,806,42,20],"src":[]},{"id":"ea7aa749-6512-4302-969f-36b5af4edf7c","name":"Tz","nick":"Tz","box":[1083,826,42,20],"src":[]},{"id":"bbad9dfe-d14f-44a3-a7c1-60dee6a173c9","name":"Rx","nick":"Rx","box":[1083,846,42,20],"src":[]},{"id":"98d9bd05-ed85-4f51-8d04-b7e1738c8881","name":"Ry","nick":"Ry","box":[1083,866,42,20],"src":[]},{"id":"398c0d62-3b88-4c85-8608-847e5f7ce3c3","name":"Rz","nick":"Rz","box":[1083,886,42,20],"src":[]}],"outputs":[{"id":"9ef68736-fc5b-4dc5-9b38-c6533669363b","name":"Support","nick":"Support","box":[1155,766,45,140],"src":[]}],"unit":"Support Rigid"},
{"kind":"component","id":"b9f8eb8d-3476-4a0f-8c33-fefc441e4de2","type":"4b6fa7fd-9388-4699-b374-b94f94583a53","name":"Gravity Load (Alpaca4d)","nick":"Own weight","desc":"Construct a Gravity Load: the model's own weight, down. A beam carries its weight as a uniform load along it, a truss half on each end, a shell or a solid shared among its nodes.","box":[1212,822,96,28],"pivot":[1262,836],"inputs":[{"id":"7736e56d-c3f9-4378-8b9e-f120b50e3364","name":"Factor","nick":"Factor","box":[1214,824,33,24],"src":[]}],"outputs":[{"id":"1e3b5e8e-6617-4a45-91ca-352536c36d39","name":"Load","nick":"Load","box":[1277,824,29,24],"src":[]}]},
